#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/id #uuid "1fed57b3-b606-5c2b-a707-2c7810dbe5d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5153" :question/problem "Expand ${( 3x + y )}^3$ using the binomial theorem." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3,$ we have  $a=3x,$ $b=y,$ and $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+y)^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3-1}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3-2}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3-3}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1)+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3x)^{3}+3\\cdot (3x)^{2}(y)+3\\cdot (3x)(y)^{2}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot ({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}y+9x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/id #uuid "be5dc7d6-ab62-55e2-8c89-4be3d49e816b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5154" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left( {3 + x} \\right)^{7}} .$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${\\left( {3 +x} \\right)^{7}},$ we have $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+x)^{7}=(\\frac{7}{0}){3}^{7}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{7}{1}){3}^{7-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{7}{2}){3}^{7-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{7}{7}){3}^{7-7}{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{4}){3}^{7-4} & =35\\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =945.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29896622-4bcb-5ad2-91d0-47c5911a619e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of an Expansion With Negative Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "c0969f12-0efa-5f9b-880f-781d8800a6f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9775" :question/problem "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${( x - 3y )}^ 5.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5,$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-3y ,$ and $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5-1}(-3y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5-2}(-3y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(x)^{5}+5(x)^{4}(-3y)+10(x)^{3}(9{y}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{5}-15{x}^{4}y+90{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb44669-6c09-5452-8903-3da761de4065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} {:answer/id #uuid "3d65452b-bf9c-5eef-a152-c7471af5d34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283116} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ae7c6f-bf08-5942-8736-6fafbec8d1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283117} {:answer/id #uuid "36ea5326-c059-54bb-afdf-108be4f45a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283118} {:answer/id #uuid "38612f8d-9461-5cbd-9b3e-9eedb6276689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38612f8d-9461-5cbd-9b3e-9eedb6276689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283119} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e9fa98-0a82-59da-8eb3-337bd9e3451a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f5f91ac-3706-5b6e-99d4-de4e4940a4d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113621" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ in the expansion of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcacdd04-262a-5bf2-b263-300d1f23a456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,608" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "19415923-a431-5737-a394-60a4412af994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,268" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283123} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdd3d9f-4a96-5362-ad4c-878a85c2aa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "756" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283124} {:answer/id #uuid "3696b022-c9bd-59ba-b079-138f4135f349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283125} {:answer/id #uuid "0558b4ce-ebc6-5aac-8359-902f5269357d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "504" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19415923-a431-5737-a394-60a4412af994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,268" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283123} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6ef5cb-6edd-5fd7-86ca-0b60348d8bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81c2dcce-4171-5e5c-a2ec-99ba650f8156" :question/math-academy-id "q-113625" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ in the expansion of $(y+3z)^{9}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(y+3z)}^{9},$ we have $a=y,$ $b=3z,$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+3z)}^{9}=(\\frac{9}{0}){y}^{9}(3z)^{0}+(\\frac{9}{1}){y}^{9-1}(3z)^{1}+(\\frac{9}{2}){y}^{9-2}(3z)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{9}{9}){y}^{9-9}(3z)^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3} & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot {3}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot 27{z}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\,268{y}^{6}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ is $2\\,268.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d80763d6-6b65-5ad9-8641-d0e0f120b387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283129} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cce20c-122b-5cfd-8d08-2e5c1b142e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283130} {:answer/id #uuid "a47a0992-c8a7-564d-a184-8857fe414b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283131} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb94883-41eb-5772-93ae-73c6e9f2104e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+120{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "2e36e0cd-54b1-57eb-853b-0bd47c75a807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80763d6-6b65-5ad9-8641-d0e0f120b387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283129} :answer-field/id #uuid "967ae736-76e4-5404-af9b-1daeb8c65f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e01d5c53-05d5-5886-b4aa-a254711880e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-12490" :question/problem "${(x+7y)}^{10}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ $n=10.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x+7y)}^{10}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10} & =(\\frac{10}{0}){(x)}^{10}{(7y)}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){(x)}^{10-1}{(7y)}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){(x)}^{10-2}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{10}+10{(x)}^{9}{(7y)}^{1}+45{(x)}^{8}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1({x}^{10})+10({x}^{9})(7y)+45({x}^{8})(49{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc6ffa5-e1ed-554e-8689-29b926432078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}+16{x}^{7}y+1024{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283136} {:answer/id #uuid "176c283e-29a8-5815-b268-d73f957ac211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}+16{x}^{7}y+108{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283137} {:answer/id #uuid "47e432a5-372d-5911-b49c-2a11e1f233c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value