:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{x}^{5}+80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283172} {:answer/id #uuid "63dd51bf-02e3-5179-9e80-333a79ab75f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+10{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283173} {:answer/id #uuid "7be26787-5a57-5eec-a648-2e819334bd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} {:answer/id #uuid "defdfbb2-b877-503a-a272-ade4c10601fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{x}^{5}-16{x}^{4}y-8{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7be26787-5a57-5eec-a648-2e819334bd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c0b104-781b-5d81-8f60-e14dc77db01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "680d95da-c64b-51ae-9fd3-1e8cabb59dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-136012" :question/problem "${(2x-y)}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2x-y)}^{5},$ we have  $a=2x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(2x-y)}^{5}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(2x)^{5}(-y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(2x)^{5-1}(-y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(2x)^{5-2}(-y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(2x)^{5}+5(2x)^{4}(-y)+10(2x)^{3}(-y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =32{x}^{5}-80{x}^{4}y+80{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29896622-4bcb-5ad2-91d0-47c5911a619e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of an Expansion With Negative Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0190e0dc-3b16-5b76-a759-53db9c2ea7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y-14{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283178} {:answer/id #uuid "176f9bb7-8865-52c8-b9a2-75f4214a8292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}+8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283179} {:answer/id #uuid "c94a9771-268f-5e04-b4ea-2a1ce834f84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283180} {:answer/id #uuid "2901f429-f79b-5e81-b52c-0921d01aa691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}-7{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283181} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f09efb-f0c0-555a-8ff2-48a1bec691be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+14{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94a9771-268f-5e04-b4ea-2a1ce834f84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283180} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d0b4ca-481b-549b-9b75-67badc8957cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c78651b2-e51e-5b1d-819a-778ab02a9f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-136015" :question/problem "${(x-y)}^{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-y)}^{8},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-y,$ $n=8.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-y)}^{8}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-y)}^{8} & =(\\frac{8}{0}){(x)}^{8}{(-y)}^{0}+(\\frac{8}{1}){(x)}^{8-1}{(-y)}^{1}+(\\frac{8}{2}){(x)}^{8-2}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{8}+8{(x)}^{7}{(-y)}^{1}+28{(x)}^{6}{(-y)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d10113d4-06cb-578d-ab16-c29371104e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283185} {:answer/id #uuid "dece2f8e-c302-5001-957b-c74705429789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283186} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba33bd7-e011-5efa-8570-092b1374c8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283187} {:answer/id #uuid "82efacab-5024-5f0b-bf65-1b2c73fbda8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283188} {:answer/id #uuid "31b96ddb-6377-5915-9b81-db79dce98d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,868" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d10113d4-06cb-578d-ab16-c29371104e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283185} :answer-field/id #uuid "5784ac6f-cc55-56ed-ad24-2bdce0e54147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29bb6817-386e-5c6e-b9b4-09a8f5774754" :question/math-academy-id "q-18624" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ in the expansion of ${(2+x)}^{15}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+x)}^{15},$ we have $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{15}=(\\frac{15}{0}){2}^{15}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{15}{1}){2}^{15-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{15}{2}){2}^{15-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{15}{15}){2}^{15-15}{x}^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{12}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12}{x}^{12}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12} & =455\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =455\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ is $3\\,640.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdd4c87e-a26f-5908-8fa9-2712e35dd3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283192} {:answer/id #uuid "63087eea-e658-5bfb-a0b5-cf69e2ed8fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41\\,160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283193} {:answer/id #uuid "5a27f094-1ec1-5f90-a98c-415759e3b9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41\\,680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283194} {:answer/id #uuid "b09ce2e5-f03f-5991-8aa4-8311cba7f2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283195} {:answer/id #uuid "4c19ca1d-a2fb-5d86-92ba-ef1273d6e485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63087eea-e658-5bfb-a0b5-cf69e2ed8fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41\\,160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283193} :answer-field/id #uuid "243d7a25-800a-5f91-aa21-aef7947bc9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5962acc2-671e-5755-bce5-267269bf9d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-18625" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(x+7y)}^{10}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){x}^{10}(7y)^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){x}^{10-1}(7y)^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){x}^{10-2}(7y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){x}^{10-10}(7y)^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3} & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot {7}^{3}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot 343{y}^{3} \\\\  & =41\\,160{x}^{7}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ is $41\\,160.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccc7ccc1-fa5c-5854-baf9-57446b8200ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098939} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9a7340-dad1-59ee-96f0-943baa160f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098940} {:answer/id #uuid "0c27bcb4-4d5e-50e3-a961-cb9140ef2dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098941} {:answer/id #uuid "15342acf-ce1d-576d-8e6b-ed1eacafb40e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098942} {:answer/id #uuid "4adf8a67-6c13-5867-ad1f-ddd1aadc64ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccc7ccc1-fa5c-5854-baf9-57446b8200ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098939} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3379b7d-3e03-5cec-8468-6ba415cd7f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfee74aa-803f-5479-96f1-16dab36aa6d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-18627" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ in the expansion of ${(x+2y)}^{8}$ is" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+2y)}^{8},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=2y,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+2y)}^{8}=(\\frac{8}{0}){x}^{8}(2y)^{0}+(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}+(\\frac{8}{2}){x}^{8-2}(2y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{8}{8}){x}^{8-8}(2y)^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}y$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{8}{1}){x}^{8-1}(2y)^{1} & =8{x}^{7}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =16{x}^{7}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}y$ is $16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29896622-4bcb-5ad2-91d0-47c5911a619e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of an Expansion With Negative Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde70f85-d134-576e-8c5a-50db852bd536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} - 12x^{5}y + 60x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099011} {:answer/id #uuid "e89525c4-89e0-52d3-82ab-1eafc9ba05a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} - 12x^{5}y + 80x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099012} {:answer/id #uuid "39f4ab49-cb68-5405-9c72-d23944d5928f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} - 12x^{5}y + 24x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099013} {:answer/id #uuid "62ec1a86-67bf-5dc3-8198-51f19671abe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} + 12x^{5}y + 1024x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099014} {:answer/id #uuid "6d274e6a-9ac1-5d58-892d-7e55a8304aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} + 12x^{5}y + 30x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde70f85-d134-576e-8c5a-50db852bd536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6} - 12x^{5}y + 60x^{4}y^{2} + …" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099011} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90ecb1c-6dcc-5061-9bcf-69d71531f03f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a56f6ef0-1192-5a7e-9865-0f892330f196" :question/math-academy-id "q-19353" :question/problem "${(x-2y)}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-2y)}^{6},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-2y,$ $n=6.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-2y)}^{6}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2y)^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0})(x)^{6}(-2y)^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1})(x)^{6-1}(-2y)^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2})(x)^{6-2}(-2y)^{2}+… \\\\  & =1(x)^{6}+6(x)^{5}(-2y)+15(x)^{4}(4{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{6}-12{x}^{5}y+60{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
